1
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:24,080
This factory initially produced all kinds of
and sizes of paper, for shop counters,

2
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,600
to make cone bags, from that old-fashioned paper.

3
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:30,960
Nowadays, it mainly focuses on packaging paper.

4
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:36,800
When the factory was founded,

5
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:41,480
the width of this machine was
ninety-centimetres,

6
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:43,640
that was the width of that ‘calandra’.

7
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:49,040
Then my father's predecessors

8
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:51,240
widened it to one metre twenty.

9
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:55,200
And we, about 40 years ago,

10
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,720
widened it again to two metres thirty.

11
00:00:58,600 --> 00:00:59,720
Because of wages,

12
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:04,320
and to remain competitive,
and also because of paper sizes,

13
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,000
that's why it was made wider.

14
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:09,960
That was around...

15
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:12,480
he bought it around...

16
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:16,000
in ‘41, bought it then, and started

17
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:18,280
operating it in ‘43.

18
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:22,960
This is a system that was used about a hundred years
ago.

19
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:26,000
Here in the paper department, where we are talking about,

20
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:28,080
seven, seven workers worked,

21
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,640
but in the past,

22
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:33,320
it wasn't ours yet,

23
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:35,080
but I knew it, I remember,

24
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:37,040
they worked,

25
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:38,680
they didn't work in the summer.

26
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:43,240
There was no power source, so they didn't work.

27
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:46,320
Later, wages had to continue to be paid,

28
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:51,000
and with the advent of ‘motorisation’ ,
 those two things came together

29
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,200
and then work continued throughout the year,
in summer and winter.

30
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:01,890
In the beginning, it didn't have millstones in that form,

31
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:06,360
later my father built the millstones into it.

32
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:15,870
You put the paper in there to recycle it,

33
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:17,760
the old paper

34
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:19,760
together with water.

35
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,470
This is an industry that comes to a standstill immediately

36
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:25,720
if the water stops flowing in here,

37
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:28,240
just like when the power goes out,

38
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:30,320
the power goes out, it's exactly the same.

39
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:36,840
Then it stays like that for a few hours,
about two hours, to dissolve.

40
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:42,640
Then it goes to a ‘refining machine’,
a cylinder or a kind of ‘refiner’.

41
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:22,950
It goes into that cylinder,
and stays there for about twenty minutes,

42
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:26,200
or half an hour,
depending on what is needed,

43
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:27,280
to refine the pulp.

44
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:19,640
Then it goes into the vat,
where it is transported further.

45
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:23,120
This is done by a wheel called the ‘Maxão’ wheel.

46
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:28,320
Nowadays, there are pumps
that can do that.

47
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:39,800
Then it goes with the water over the plates,

48
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:43,000
it flows with the water over the plates.

49
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:48,800
The pulp and the water...
you can't see whether there is paper in it or not.

50
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,140
Then it goes into a drum

51
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:12,000
with a cylinder inside that rotates,
and it's closed.

52
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:18,640
It has a pump that sucks the water out,
so that it is always dry on the inside,

53
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:22,640
that drum that is cylindrical, and the paper
separates from the water.

54
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:27,640
And that's how the paper is formed,
over and over again.

55
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:31,230
That motor is the most important part.

56
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:32,440
If it stops,

57
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:35,600
that causes the water to be sucked in.

58
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,120
And that creates pressure

59
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:40,280
which makes the paper stick to the roll.

60
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,480
And then, where the paper sticks
to it, is wool.

61
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:50,360
And the paper, the pulp, sticks to the wool,
and leaves the roll,

62
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:53,680
that roll is made of metal,
of copper,

63
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:57,680
and then it comes onto this ‘calandra’,
and we tension it.

64
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:00,120
It has a wooden clamp underneath,

65
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:05,080
with rubber on the outside,
and above that it is made of iron.

66
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,080
And we have to stretch it,

67
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:11,400
to make the paper as dry as possible,
otherwise the paper breaks,

68
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:15,280
it becomes a pile of wet paper
that cannot be transported.

69
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:18,520
We have to stretch it on both sides
to squeeze out as much water as possible

70
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:20,240
and get all the paper half dry.

71
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:23,440
The factory ran all day,
there was no lunch break,

72
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:26,080
they worked continuously for eight hours.

73
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:28,920
All the time was counted,
even when they ate.

74
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:58,320
Here we have to turn the ‘sarilho’,

75
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:00,280
and we have to attach the paper to the roll again

76
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:02,000
so as not to lose any time,

77
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,040
as quickly as possible, so that
as little paper as possible falls on the floor,

78
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:06,040
to save as much as possible.

79
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:08,080
We have to do that as quickly as we can,
turn that ‘sarilho’.

80
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:44,120
We saw it through with a rotating disc

81
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:46,000
and when it's cut through

82
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:48,440
it has to be done by hand, with a saw.

83
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:04,960
With a good sharp saw (without teeth)

84
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:07,120
so that it's not such hard work.

85
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:09,600
The sharper the saw,

86
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:11,240
the less it costs to cut the paper.

87
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:13,280
and then the girls hang it up above.

88
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,040
Then this cart goes to the lift,

89
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:15,080
the paper on the racks.

90
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:19,280
That's their job, hanging up the paper.

91
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:22,400
And that's quite heavy work too.

92
00:11:24,040 --> 00:11:27,480
They have to lift the racks by hand,
as you may have already seen.

93
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:33,480
with a stick and a hook,
and then lift them up by hand.

94
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:34,320
That's pretty heavy.

95
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:38,800
And they're not that young anymore,
they're already in their fifties.

96
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:41,760
That's quite heavy work.

97
00:12:32,470 --> 00:12:33,510
We put the paper on the table

98
00:12:34,510 --> 00:12:35,510
and on the table we fold it

99
00:12:35,990 --> 00:12:37,000
fold it

100
00:12:36,990 --> 00:12:38,150
and hang it on the rack,

101
00:12:39,150 --> 00:12:40,790
on the cross, and then on the rack.

102
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,080
And then we hoist the rack up.

103
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:46,760
We hoist the racks higher and higher,
until we have a gallery full.

104
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:48,500
A gallery has six racks.

105
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:50,940
Then we move on to the next gallery.

106
00:12:51,140 --> 00:12:55,000
We go from gallery to gallery,
each gallery has six racks.

107
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:25,800
My name is Silvina Pinto,
I am 55 years old.

108
00:14:26,140 --> 00:14:28,340
If necessary, I come here to help my sister

109
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:30,420
lift the racks upstairs.

110
00:14:30,700 --> 00:14:32,260
This work is very hard,

111
00:14:32,780 --> 00:14:33,420
very hard.

112
00:19:36,170 --> 00:19:37,240
And that's how the paper is.

113
00:19:37,250 --> 00:19:40,000
Then they make bales with this press here,

114
00:19:40,050 --> 00:19:42,160
The girls do that too.

115
00:19:42,170 --> 00:19:44,720
A bale weighs 50 kilos,
every bale weighs 50 kilos.

116
00:19:45,010 --> 00:19:46,250
They put a wire on each side.

117
00:19:47,060 --> 00:19:47,500
And then

118
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:49,860
they tighten the press with that iron,

119
00:19:50,380 --> 00:19:52,800
they tighten the press.

120
00:19:52,780 --> 00:19:54,400
And then they close the bale and take it off the press,

121
00:19:54,580 --> 00:19:56,440
to put it on the pile.

122
00:21:36,930 --> 00:21:39,330
In the past, the bales weighed 100 kilos,

123
00:21:40,890 --> 00:21:42,850
then they became 50 kilos,

124
00:21:43,330 --> 00:21:45,970
a long time ago already they became 50 kilos,

125
00:21:46,450 --> 00:21:48,960
because people are getting weaker

126
00:21:48,970 --> 00:21:52,040
and now they weigh about 25 kilos.

127
00:21:53,140 --> 00:21:54,620
Their strength has diminished.

129
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:00,720
If necessary for an order

130
00:23:01,080 --> 00:23:04,920
the boss comes with his delivery van
and we help with the loading,

131
00:23:05,240 --> 00:23:06,280
with the bales on our heads.

132
00:23:07,140 --> 00:23:10,240
Not in my case,
but the girls do that.

133
00:23:10,220 --> 00:23:16,000
Tie them down, and off they go.
If it rains, we have to put a tarpaulin over them,

134
00:23:15,980 --> 00:23:16,900
so that the paper doesn't get wet,

135
00:23:17,780 --> 00:23:19,980
otherwise it goes like this, in the open air.

136
00:23:39,980 --> 00:23:40,940
And it seems to me...

137
00:23:42,420 --> 00:23:44,370
that the story has been told.

